<h1>Getting started with SAShiny</h1>

<p>SAShiny lets an <strong>unchanged SAS program</strong> act as the server of
a Shiny app. A sidecar YAML contract declares the program's inputs and outputs;
SAShiny validates the inputs, safely passes them to SAS as macro variables,
submits the program verbatim, and collects the results.</p>

<p>This vignette walks through the core workflow using the
<strong>mock engine</strong>, which needs no SAS installation.</p>

<h2>The contract</h2>

<p>A SAShiny program is a pair of files:</p>
<ul>
  <li><code>analysis.sas</code> — your SAS program (never modified).</li>
  <li><code>analysis.sashiny.yml</code> — the contract declaring inputs and
    outputs.</li>
</ul>

<!--begin.rcode
library(SAShiny)

# Write a minimal contract and program to a temporary directory.
dir <- tempfile("sashiny-")
dir.create(dir)
writeLines(c(
  "sashiny: 1",
  "program: program.sas",
  "title: Minimal",
  "outputs:",
  "  log:",
  "    type: log"
), file.path(dir, "contract.sashiny.yml"))
writeLines("proc print data=sashelp.class; run;", file.path(dir, "program.sas"))
end.rcode-->

<h2>Load and validate</h2>

<!--begin.rcode
contract <- sas_contract(file.path(dir, "contract.sashiny.yml"))
issues <- sas_validate(contract)
length(issues)  # 0 means valid
end.rcode-->

<h2>Run against the mock engine</h2>

<!--begin.rcode
engine <- sas_engine_mock()
result <- sas_run(contract, list(), engine)
result$status
end.rcode-->

<h2>Connection selection</h2>

<p>For live SAS, choose one of three backends. The consumer supplies the
connection details; credentials come from <code>~/.authinfo</code>.</p>

<!--begin.rcode
oda <- sas_connect("oda", region = "us1")
oda$cfg$authkey
end.rcode-->

<h2>Next steps</h2>

<p>See the <code>spec/CONTRACT.md</code> file in the source repository for the
full contract reference, and <code>docs/HANDOFF.md</code> for the path to
CRAN/PyPI.</p>

